WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.720
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:13.500
In 2021, scientists at Google had an absolutely&nbsp;
insane idea. Do you see this photo? Yes? They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:21.360
said, now, let’s give this to the AI, and ask&nbsp;
it to fly into this photo. Insanity. And yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:26.820
it worked like this. Absolutely&nbsp;
amazing. It was not given a video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:32.040
and not even a collection of&nbsp;
photos. Just this one photo.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:40.020
However, there is a problem. Do you see the&nbsp;
problem? Well, this is the problem. Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:46.320
if we look for a while, it has to come up&nbsp;
with newer and newer content, and over time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:51.600
the consistency of the results suffer.&nbsp;
We don’t get one coherent landscape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:56.940
but something that morphs into&nbsp;
something else not connected to it.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:05.580
And today, this is why I am so excited to show&nbsp;
you NVIDIA’s new paper on video generation. Let’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:11.880
see…yes, they promise exactly what we are&nbsp;
looking for. Better long-term consistency!

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:18.180
So first, let’s see what previous techniques&nbsp;
can do and then, what this new one can.&nbsp;

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:25.560
This is an excellent test case. Now, you may&nbsp;
be asking Károly, what in the world is this?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:32.520
And I say that is exactly the point.&nbsp;
MoCoGAN-HD is a technique from 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:38.220
from just 2 years ago, and we cannot&nbsp;
even tell what it is trying to do.

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:46.860
Here is a later work from 2022, the time-agnostic&nbsp;
time-sensitive transformer TATS in short. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:53.340
isn’t great either, but now, at least we see&nbsp;
what it is trying to do. And that is rendering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:59.940
clouds. It starts out reasonably well, but as&nbsp;
you see, the results quickly start degrading,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:07.500
and that is the point of today’s video.&nbsp;
Long-term consistency is super difficult. DiGAN,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:11.880
another work from last year does&nbsp;
way better than these previous two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:17.760
great progress, but as you see,&nbsp;
the video is full of artifacts.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:23.340
And now, hold on to your papers Fellow&nbsp;
Scholars and let’s see the new technique!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:30.900
Holy mother of papers! Do you see that?&nbsp;
It is leaps and bounds beyond any of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:37.140
techniques. And all this just one more&nbsp;
paper down the line. How cool is that?

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:43.860
I don’t know about you, but I am itching to see&nbsp;
more examples. Let’s compare against StyleGAN-V,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:50.280
one of the state of the art techniques out there&nbsp;
for this task. First, horseback riding. I must&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:57.240
say it is pretty incredible that today, an AI is&nbsp;
capable of creating a video like this. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:05.820
there are issues. Things morph in and out of&nbsp;
existence, and did you notice? This doesn’t really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:13.440
synthesize new scenery, it just repeats this jump&nbsp;
over and over. Now let’s see the new technique.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:21.120
Wow! Now we’re talking! After that first jump,&nbsp;
we get new scenery as we enter the forest.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:29.100
But it has even more advantages. Look, when&nbsp;
synthesizing a landscape, I have a much better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:34.260
sense of camera motion and rotation with the&nbsp;
new technique than with the previous one.

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:42.120
And it can do something even better too. Yes,&nbsp;
this is going to be everyone’s favorite. Super&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:49.260
resolution. What is that? The enhance thing.&nbsp;
First, it generates a coarse video, and then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:56.820
adds more and more detail to it until it becomes&nbsp;
a much sharper, higher resolution video. Just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.820 --> 00:04:02.460
look at the difference. It takes no more than&nbsp;
a heap of pixels that looks like a computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:09.840
game from 25 years ago, and it understands what&nbsp;
this scene is meant to portray, and it creates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:16.980
this detailed version of it, and I would argue it&nbsp;
would do at least as well as a human would. Wow.

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:23.040
So, clearly, the new technique seems way&nbsp;
better, but this is a research paper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:31.260
in which scientists at NVIDIA also have to show&nbsp;
mathematically that it is better. So how do they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:37.560
do it? How do they measure the differences? Well,&nbsp;
first, at the risk of simplifying the measurement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:45.720
we show these videos to a neural network that&nbsp;
somewhat mimics the perception of humans. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:54.540
the lower the score it gives, the better. And…oh&nbsp;
my goodness. Look at that. Apart from a few, super&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.540 --> 00:05:01.380
low-resolution cases, this new technique is so&nbsp;
much better than the previous methods. Incredible.

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:08.340
But there is another way of measuring which&nbsp;
is exactly better, and even more importantly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:15.360
by how much. And that is, of course,&nbsp;
showing these videos to humans and asking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:20.640
them. In the user study, 500 people&nbsp;
were asked which is more realistic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:28.440
and about 80-85% of the people chose the new&nbsp;
technique over StyleGAN-V, the previous one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:35.880
That is incredible. And once again, all this&nbsp;
progress in AI research in less than a year.

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:42.660
Now, clearly the new technique is not&nbsp;
perfect either, it has artifacts, morphing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:50.460
and other issues as well. However, this is&nbsp;
incredible progress in just one paper. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:57.420
as always, as a wise Fellow Scholar, please invoke&nbsp;
the First Law of Papers, which says that research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.420 --> 00:06:03.660
is a process. Do not look at where we are, look&nbsp;
at where we will be two more papers down the line.

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:09.060
So, finally, better long-term&nbsp;
consistency for AI-generated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:14.340
videos. What a time to be alive!&nbsp;
Also, good news! The source code,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:22.620
datasets and pretrained models for this paper&nbsp;
are available, so let the experiments begin! So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:27.480
what video you would generate using this&nbsp;
technique? Let me know in the comments below!

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:30.540
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

